// Test: every arithmetic operator with an Int and a Float, in both orders.
//
// Type promotion is where two engines can agree on the arithmetic and still
// disagree on the answer, because the question is which of the two types the
// result takes and when the conversion happens. `7 / 2` is 3 and `7 / 2.0` is
// 3.5, and nothing in the source says so except the type of a literal.

// ── Addition ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
>>(3 + 2) ¶
>>(3 + 2.0) ¶
>>(3.0 + 2) ¶
>>(3.0 + 2.0) ¶
>>(3 + 0.5) ¶
>>(-3 + 0.5) ¶
>>(3 + -0.5) ¶

// ── Subtraction, in both orders (it does not commute) ───────────────────────
>>(3 - 2) ¶
>>(3 - 2.0) ¶
>>(3.0 - 2) ¶
>>(2 - 3.0) ¶
>>(2.0 - 3) ¶
>>(0 - 0.5) ¶
>>(0.5 - 1) ¶

// ── Multiplication ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
>>(3 * 2) ¶
>>(3 * 2.0) ¶
>>(3.0 * 2) ¶
>>(3 * 0.5) ¶
>>(0.5 * 3) ¶
>>(-3 * 0.5) ¶
>>(3 * 0.0) ¶
>>(3.0 * 0) ¶

// ── Division — the operator whose type changes the answer ───────────────────
// Int / Int truncates; one Float on either side makes it exact.
>>(7 / 2) ¶
>>(7 / 2.0) ¶
>>(7.0 / 2) ¶
>>(7.0 / 2.0) ¶
>>(6 / 3) ¶
>>(6 / 3.0) ¶
>>(1 / 2) ¶
>>(1 / 2.0) ¶
>>(1.0 / 2) ¶

// Truncation of a negative quotient: toward zero, not toward −∞.
>>(-7 / 2) ¶
>>(7 / -2) ¶
>>(-7 / -2) ¶
>>(-7 / 2.0) ¶
>>(-7.0 / 2) ¶

// ── Modulo, including the sign of the remainder ─────────────────────────────
>>(7 % 2) ¶
>>(7 % 2.0) ¶
>>(7.0 % 2) ¶
>>(7.0 % 2.0) ¶
>>(-7 % 2) ¶
>>(7 % -2) ¶
>>(-7 % -2) ¶
>>(-7.0 % 2) ¶
>>(7 % -2.0) ¶
>>(7.5 % 2) ¶
>>(7 % 2.5) ¶

// ── Exponentiation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
>>(2 ^ 3) ¶
>>(2 ^ 3.0) ¶
>>(2.0 ^ 3) ¶
>>(2.0 ^ 3.0) ¶
>>(2 ^ 0) ¶
>>(2.0 ^ 0) ¶
>>(2 ^ 0.5) ¶
>>(4.0 ^ 0.5) ¶
>>(2 ^ -2) ¶
>>(2.0 ^ -2) ¶
>>(-2 ^ 3) ¶
>>(-2.0 ^ 3) ¶
>>(0 ^ 0) ¶
>>(0.0 ^ 0) ¶
>>(1 ^ 0.5) ¶

// A fractional power of a negative base has no real answer.
>>(-8.0 ^ 0.5) ¶

// ── The result type, stated rather than inferred from how it prints ─────────
// `3 + 2.0` is 5 and `3 + 2` is 5; only `#?` tells them apart.
>>((3 + 2)#?) ¶
>>((3 + 2.0)#?) ¶
>>((7 / 2)#?) ¶
>>((7 / 2.0)#?) ¶
>>((2 ^ 3)#?) ¶
>>((2 ^ 3.0)#?) ¶
>>((2 ^ -2)#?) ¶
>>((7 % 2)#?) ¶
>>((7 % 2.0)#?) ¶

// ── Comparison across the two types ─────────────────────────────────────────
>>(3 == 3.0) ¶
>>(3 < 3.5) ¶
>>(3.5 < 4) ¶
>>(3 <= 3.0) ¶
>>(3 >= 3.0) ¶
>>(3 <> 3.0) ¶
>>(2.5 > 2) ¶

// ── Division and modulo by zero keep their rules across the promotion ───────
!? { >>(1 / 0) ¶ }     :! ##Div { >> "int div zero" ¶ }
!? { >>(1 / 0.0) ¶ }   :! ##Div { >> "float div zero" ¶ }
!? { >>(1.0 / 0) ¶ }   :! ##Div { >> "float div int zero" ¶ }
!? { >>(1 % 0) ¶ }     :! ##Div { >> "int mod zero" ¶ }
!? { >>(1 % 0.0) ¶ }   :! ##Div { >> "float mod zero" ¶ }
!? { >>(1.0 % 0) ¶ }   :! ##Div { >> "float mod int zero" ¶ }

>> "end" ¶
